```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a dead FRR service on leaf_router_0_1, not a problem on
spine_router_0_0 as the team suspected. The frr.service on leaf_router_0_1 is
"inactive (dead)" — meaning bgpd (and zebra) never started on that device.
Because bgpd is down, leaf_router_0_1 cannot bring up the two eBGP sessions it
needs: one to spine_router_0_0 (172.16.0.6 ↔ 172.16.0.7) and one to
spine_router_0_1 (172.16.0.10 ↔ 172.16.0.11). Both spines show exactly those
peer addresses stuck in the BGP "Active" state, confirming no TCP session was
ever established from the leaf side. As a direct consequence, the prefix
10.0.1.0/24 (pc_0_1's subnet, connected on leaf_router_0_1:eth2) is never
advertised into the BGP fabric. Neither spine nor super_spine_router_0 carries
a BGP route for 10.0.1.0/24; super_spine's RIB contains only 10.0.0.0/24.
leaf_router_0_0 therefore has no forwarding entry for 10.0.1.2 and correctly
returns ICMP Destination Net Unreachable back to pc_0_0, which matches the
reported symptom exactly. spine_router_0_0 is healthy: its sessions to
super_spine and leaf_router_0_0 are both Established and exchanging prefixes.
The root cause is that the FRR service was never started (or crashed without
restart) on leaf_router_0_1. The corrective fix is to restart FRR on
leaf_router_0_1 ("systemctl start frr"); /etc/frr/daemons already has bgpd=yes
and the BGP configuration is in place, so once the service starts, bgpd will
establish sessions with both spines and advertise 10.0.1.0/24, restoring
end-to-end reachability between pc_0_0 and pc_0_1.
=== END ===
